# Gurbalib development workflow

## Setup (first time)

1. `perl scripts/create_data_dirs`
2. Build and install DGD from https://github.com/dworkin/dgd
3. `cp mud.dgd.examp mud.dgd` and edit paths
4. `cp lib/kernel/include/local_config.h.default lib/kernel/include/local_config.h`
5. `./bin/startmud` (after copying/configuring `scripts/startmud`)

See `doc/INSTALLING` and `doc/FAQ.md`.

## Edit-test cycle (running mud)

1. Edit the `.c` file
2. In-game as wizard: **`update /path/to/file.c`**
3. For commands: **`rehash`** after adding new command files
4. Use **`check <file>`** to validate syntax and inheritance
5. Use **`clone <file>`** to test objects; **`dest <object>`** to clean up

## Debugging

| Problem | Check |
|---------|-------|
| Mud exits silently | `lib/logs/gurba-driver.log` |
| Syntax/compile error on boot | Error message names file and line; fix and restart |
| `local_config.h` missing | Copy from `.default`, set `IMUD_NAME` and mud name macros |
| New command not found | Run `rehash` as admin |
| First login not admin | `scripts/clean_mud` and restart (first player gets admin) |

## Safe editing boundaries

| Safe to edit freely | Edit with care | Avoid unless necessary |
|---------------------|----------------|------------------------|
| `lib/domains/` | `lib/std/modules/` | `lib/kernel/` |
| Area-specific content | `lib/std/` base classes | `lib/sys/daemons/` |
| New commands in `lib/cmds/` | `lib/sys/cmds/` | Driver source |

## Scripts

- `scripts/indent_it` — format LPC (review output)
- `scripts/check_doc` — validate man page format for commands
- `scripts/clean_mud` — wipe player data for fresh start
- `scripts/backup_lib` — backup before large changes

## Upgrade path

When pulling mudlib updates, read `doc/UPGRADING` before merging.

## AI editing guidelines

- Read the nearest existing file of the same type before writing new code
- Prefer minimal diffs — do not refactor unrelated code in the same file
- Do not remove or rewrite shipped example domains unless asked
- Verify new exit paths and clone paths point to files that exist or are created in the same change
- After structural changes (new modules, daemons), mention that a **full mud restart** may be required, not just `update`
